#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89e4f702-762c-5e81-ac89-0bf727b0772d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - Φ(1.43)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061069} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4cfca9-71ea-57a5-b13b-709f1988258e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - Φ(1.20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061070} {:answer/id #uuid "cc768479-f924-56a3-9d7d-44fe6773e1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - Φ(1.22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061071} {:answer/id #uuid "1eeb3e96-523a-5e45-a8de-0d325436464f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - Φ(0.98)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061072} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7454d7-31cf-5190-9701-fd27808b4500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - Φ(1.19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061073}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d63ce57d-1cf8-5051-aaa9-78097d123d4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061068}] :question/id #uuid "b96b003a-e439-541e-b925-61b08f33a43d" :question/math-academy-id "q-110431" :question/problem "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n = 50$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $2.2$.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1314" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498820} {:answer/id #uuid "407cfaea-b447-5e42-a30b-1d2cd829a0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498878} {:answer/id #uuid "a63f4b53-b75c-5bc8-8070-14f83b495105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2641" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499094} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ceab23-5225-54d0-8603-cea96aaf2b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1562" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499163} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3a2118-ab8a-5090-8fc2-f6b42b9b4b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1314" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498820} :answer-field/id #uuid "72921358-da9c-5842-82f0-b8015a6f19c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc097a1b-4231-5bd2-b0eb-22f13d9b5569" :question/math-academy-id "q-143501" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =1.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.25,\\frac{0.1}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.25,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.2-1.25}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.12)=0.1314.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =Φ(-1.12) \\\\  & =0.1314.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a997faa-836e-5f83-9264-4f0804fb7e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427599} {:answer/id #uuid "538024b2-4701-5428-8d5e-360b49e72e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427601} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe5d3c3-8e7b-53fe-9eb0-ef18302db68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4981" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427613} {:answer/id #uuid "d7027add-1387-5c0f-b716-e26792b9321e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427614} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fe171c-a45f-55be-b10d-8947252fe379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7027add-1387-5c0f-b716-e26792b9321e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427614} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f33ee8f-8a4d-5863-8709-f1963fc40af1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d0d65d5-ccab-55b5-8cd5-5240c8c44547" :question/math-academy-id "q-143506" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{55}$ be a random sample of size $n=55$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.11,$ calculate $P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ | $\\,0.9875\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =55,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{20}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{20} \\\\  & =1.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.11.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.6,\\frac{0.11}{55}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.6,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7) & =P(\\frac{1.6-1.6}{\\sqrt{0.002}}<Z<\\frac{1.7-1.6}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<2.24) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.24)-P(Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(2.24)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(2.24)=0.9875,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution, $Φ(0)=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.6<\\overset{―}{X}<1.7) & =Φ(2.24)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =0.9875-0.5 \\\\  & =0.4875.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9992" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498846} {:answer/id #uuid "6de957fe-2080-57db-b272-b3b2f6646ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498974} {:answer/id #uuid "77e88625-4c75-5215-8f5a-0c85b4e5a671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498999} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f8b3c7-8fde-5c2f-927e-132d799da466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8976" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499091} {:answer/id #uuid "f7306d02-503e-55d9-b966-b99018652bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9992" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498846} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5ea7fd-d63e-51a1-9d3e-f96a0fcebecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cad4258a-bb8c-5f12-a390-574af230e1b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143509" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-3.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0012\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7b7bfd-bb55-564e-9903-0d5b6e678486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7173" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498759} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb173f3-e4b4-5ccd-a8d7-911db39988bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7985" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498938} {:answer/id #uuid "6a74c583-3769-5c0f-b18c-c5ebd3d2f5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7713" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498971} {:answer/id #uuid "abe25544-5aa0-54fe-bd59-001ab2d00a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499109} {:answer/id #uuid "b0081045-ba39-5996-b207-6c0dcb67bc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7333" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0081045-ba39-5996-b207-6c0dcb67bc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7333" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499118} :answer-field/id #uuid "851c3373-8dc4-5b32-bf52-e856ed7d70a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f56d1332-6e08-590b-aa4f-b85bae891009" :question/math-academy-id "q-143517" :question/problem "| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3.6$ and $4.2.$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7580\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7611\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7642\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7881\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7910\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7939\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8159\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8186\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8212\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0446\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0436\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0548\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0537\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0668\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0655\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0643\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=128,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.4+1+0.6+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.25)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4+4+3.6+16)-16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{8}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.0625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & ≈-P(\\frac{3.6-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}<Z<\\frac{4.2-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}) \\\\  & ≈-P(-1.6<Z<0.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.8)-P(Z<-1.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.8)=0.7881, \\\\ Φ(-1.6)=0.0548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.0548 \\\\  & =0.7333.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
